WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.000 line:84%
Welcome to the continuing
series of Academy Dialogues,

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.350 line:90%
It Starts With Us.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:05.880 line:84%
We are continuing
our conversations

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:09.960 line:84%
about race, ethnicity,
history, gender, opportunity,

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.170 line:84%
and the art of filmmaking,
hosted by the Academy.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.810 line:84%
Moderating our conversation
today, arts and culture

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:19.410 line:84%
journalist and radio host
Elvis Mitchell, in conversation

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.540 line:84%
with Mexican director
and screenwriter

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:26.460 line:84%
Rodrigo Garcia, casting director
Julia Kim, and veteran casting

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.070 line:90%
director Victoria Thomas.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.480 line:84%
We look forward to
your conversation.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:31.980 line:90%
Thank you.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.980 line:84%
So here's the question I
want to start this off with.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.570 line:84%
Just because it's something
I've been thinking about, just

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.420 line:84%
trying to figure out a
way to kick this off.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:41.850 line:84%
If there is a piece of
sort of colorless, color,

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.320 line:84%
interesting color
casting that made

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.770 line:84%
you have a hope for what
movies could be-- because I'll

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.220 line:90%
tell you mine, for example.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.920 line:84%
Seeing Billy Dee Williams in
Tim Burton's Batman, because he

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.110 line:84%
played Harvey Dent,
which made me think,

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.700 line:84%
maybe he'll get
to play Two-Face.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.680 line:90%
And of course, he didn't.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.320 line:84%
But anyway, I just wondered
if there's something like that

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.947 line:84%
that you guys saw in your lives
that made you think, well,

00:01:02.947 --> 00:01:05.280 line:84%
maybe movies-- because we get
so much of the information

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.640 line:84%
about ourselves as people
in the world from movies.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.890 line:84%
So I just wondered if there's
any moment that occurred

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.220 line:90%
to any of you guys like that?

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:18.360 line:84%
The most-- it was a film called
Searching where John Cho played

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.490 line:84%
a father, and it
had nothing to do

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:23.520 line:84%
with him playing
an Asian character,

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.270 line:84%
but really just about a father
whose concerns and worries

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:32.190 line:84%
about his daughter were common,
no matter what color you were.

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:37.680 line:84%
And that was a monumental
moment to see that breakthrough

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.010 line:90%
occur in a major film.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.080 line:84%
I think it might be
obvious, but I mean,

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:49.900 line:84%
I guess I would just
say Hamilton, where

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:55.410 line:84%
the diversification
of that cast,

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:59.040 line:84%
and obviously portraying
white characters

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.370 line:84%
with Puerto Ricans or
black people, or whatever,

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:06.255 line:84%
it kind of brought
a larger truth

00:02:06.255 --> 00:02:08.630 line:84%
to what they were trying to
do with the story of Hamilton

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:11.360 line:90%
anyway.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:18.050 line:84%
So it didn't feel so
self-conscious to me,

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:21.170 line:84%
because I think it was
getting at a bigger

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:24.390 line:84%
truth about the
creation of our country.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.890 line:84%
So I mean, that would be kind
of an obvious one for me.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:32.090 line:84%
I think, generally,
you know, you're always

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:36.200 line:84%
very thankful to see people
of color playing roles

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.480 line:84%
that are unexpected
and that are not

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.090 line:84%
about the ethnicity
itself, that don't have a--

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:44.660 line:84%
I mean, some movies, of course,
speak about the ethnicity

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:46.430 line:90%
itself.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:49.160 line:84%
But just like Julia
said, when a character

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.830 line:90%
is everything at the same time.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:54.560 line:84%
I think to say
colorblind casting,

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.570 line:84%
there's something
imprecise about it,

00:02:56.570 --> 00:03:00.530 line:84%
because who you are, including
your ethnicity and your color,

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.320 line:84%
always means something,
and will mean something

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:04.550 line:90%
to the character.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:09.380 line:84%
So it's when something
comes together

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:13.550 line:84%
in a character for who they
are, but of course, there's

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.250 line:84%
no denying also who they
are ethnically and racially.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:20.750 line:84%
I think those are the
characters that stand out.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.000 line:84%
I can't give you
examples because my brain

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.496 line:90%
is at that stage now.

00:03:24.496 --> 00:03:25.450 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:27.920 line:84%
We'll go off of that
stage into another stage.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.110 line:84%
But because what
we're talking about,

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.930 line:84%
and what you said so
beautifully, Julia, is

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:34.130 line:84%
that I think you
guys all want to do

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:39.170 line:84%
is create a tone where casting
is subtext rather than text,

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:41.270 line:84%
so we see these things,
and the identifiers

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:44.240 line:84%
become the character
and [INAUDIBLE] as much

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.130 line:90%
as the ethnicity, right?

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.500 line:90%
Absolutely.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:52.760 line:84%
I think we're on that
kind of trend anyways.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:54.740 line:84%
I think we're seeing
more and more of it.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:57.380 line:90%
So it's really encouraging.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.710 line:84%
But there's probably still
more work to be done.

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:02.656 line:84%
And I'm sure we'll
talk about it.

00:04:02.656 --> 00:04:05.690 line:84%
I mean, I hope we do,
because especially in light

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:11.330 line:84%
of the recent Academy inclusion
changes for Best Picture,

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:14.630 line:84%
I guess I want to get your
guys', get your takes on what

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.250 line:84%
you thought when
you heard that news,

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:18.795 line:84%
and what you think it
might mean for the future.

00:04:18.795 --> 00:04:22.550 line:84%
And let's start
with you, Rodrigo.

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:27.230 line:84%
I mean, I think it was
an excellent measure.

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:29.100 line:90%
It was overdue.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.670 line:84%
I think, obviously
it is of this moment.

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:35.600 line:84%
But the Academy has been moving
in the direction of inclusion

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.730 line:90%
over the last few years.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:42.950 line:84%
And I also think, you know,
there was a very good job done

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:44.940 line:84%
of doing something
that's specific,

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:48.780 line:84%
but also in its own
way also inclusive,

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:51.710 line:84%
meaning it wasn't going
to shackle filmmakers.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:54.380 line:84%
There are many
ways to contribute

00:04:54.380 --> 00:05:02.390 line:84%
to moving in the direction
of inclusion and of diversity

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:09.730 line:84%
without tying the hands of
filmmakers in such a way where

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:10.840 line:90%
it would just be crazy.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:15.040 line:84%
I think it was done in
a very appropriate way.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.190 line:84%
And I think it was
going to yield results.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:19.037 line:90%
It better.

00:05:19.037 --> 00:05:19.870 line:90%
I'm sorry, go ahead.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:21.287 line:84%
Vicky, what were
you going to say?

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:23.810 line:90%
Yeah, I thought--

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:29.770 line:84%
I know there, beside the Best
Picture category requirements,

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:32.710 line:84%
now, I thought the mentorship
and the internships

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:38.380 line:84%
are a really important
part of instituting,

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:42.340 line:84%
because people of
color don't necessarily

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:45.220 line:84%
have either family backgrounds
being in the business,

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:48.580 line:84%
or come from a line
of my dad was an AD

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:51.090 line:84%
and my mom was a
costume designer.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:57.910 line:84%
It's not as frequent, I think,
in communities of color.

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:00.710 line:84%
I did feel, I have to
say, when I saw the--

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:02.860 line:84%
and I haven't read
it to the letter,

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:09.740 line:84%
but when I saw the requirements
for Best Picture and the one--

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:16.480 line:84%
the one actor of
color be in the--

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:17.800 line:90%
yeah.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.230 line:84%
I just-- I said to
myself, what if I

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:22.810 line:84%
want to nominate a
movie about the Amish?

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:25.210 line:84%
I mean, that's-- I mean,
I kind of had a little bit

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.130 line:90%
of a hesitation about that one.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:30.190 line:84%
I can speak to that, Elvis,
if I may, because I was,

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:31.960 line:84%
like you mentioned,
I'm on the board.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:33.460 line:84%
So I was, you
know, at least part

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:37.240 line:90%
of the final processes in that.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:40.000 line:84%
I think, you know, you read
precisely the one regarding

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:40.690 line:90%
acting.

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:48.490 line:84%
But I think that these
rules allow for you to--

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:52.450 line:84%
for your production to have
inclusion in different areas,

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:57.370 line:84%
that include everything from
casting to behind the camera

00:06:57.370 --> 00:07:00.940 line:84%
to the post-production to the
studio to the distributor.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:02.770 line:90%
There are many ways to do it.

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:07.300 line:84%
And things were discussed
like you suggested.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:11.175 line:84%
Without betraying
anything, the example

00:07:11.175 --> 00:07:12.550 line:84%
did come up, well,
what if you're

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:15.640 line:84%
making a movie in Iceland
about Icelandic people?

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:23.590 line:84%
But I think the rules are
not loose, but broad enough

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:24.850 line:90%
to include that.

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:26.350 line:84%
And I think there
will also probably

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:35.210 line:84%
be a process by which particular
examples can be addressed,

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:39.880 line:84%
so that we don't end up
excluding that kind of movie.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:45.120 line:84%
I read it over, and I
applauded the effort.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:52.080 line:84%
I thought the requirements gave
a lot of space for flexibility.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:55.770 line:84%
And like you said, Rodrigo,
it's not just the acting part.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:57.990 line:90%
It's the material.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:00.450 line:90%
It's the key department heads.

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:02.100 line:90%
It's the crew.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:05.460 line:84%
So the comments that I read
felt a little knee jerk

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:10.410 line:84%
reaction oriented, but I
don't think the Academy

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:13.200 line:90%
is looking to limit creativity.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.120 line:84%
I don't think the
Academy was looking

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:21.480 line:84%
to look for reasons
to disqualify a film.

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.810 line:84%
I think what they were trying
to do is spread the love.

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:29.610 line:84%
And also, I think there
is a lot of diversity

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:33.159 line:84%
and eclectic people entering
our business on a daily basis.

00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:35.730 line:84%
So I feel like you'd almost
have to find a reason

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:39.390 line:84%
not to meet two out of the
four requirements in this day

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:39.950 line:90%
and age.

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:43.260 line:84%
But yeah, I think it's
a process in motion

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.410 line:90%
with really good intentions.

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:47.850 line:84%
Because what we're
talking about is,

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:49.485 line:90%
we're talking from the top down.

00:08:49.485 --> 00:08:51.610 line:84%
It feels like there haven't
been that many changes.

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:54.660 line:84%
But for you guys,
as creative people,

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:58.320 line:84%
it feels like that world has
kind of always been open,

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:02.007 line:84%
and is now just almost impatient
about saying, let's move

00:09:02.007 --> 00:09:02.840 line:90%
on to the next step.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:04.990 line:90%
Let's have John Cho play a dad.

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:07.967 line:84%
And yet must not be the
first thing we learn

00:09:07.967 --> 00:09:10.050 line:84%
or the first thing that's
spoken about, literally,

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:11.520 line:90%
because that's what happens.

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:13.950 line:84%
So often characters of
color will basically

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:17.050 line:84%
tell you in the first of
their lines, oh, by the way.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:20.840 line:84%
So as if the audience can't
actually suss this out.

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:23.730 line:84%
And I'm wondering if that kind
of bedrock change of being

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:27.540 line:84%
able to get people in
casts who get us past that,

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:30.750 line:84%
and make these movie
look more like the world,

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:34.530 line:84%
are even more wholesale than
they did even five years ago?

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:36.750 line:84%
I think that it
needs to look more

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:39.010 line:90%
like the world, what I see.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:40.140 line:90%
And I do see it.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:43.350 line:84%
I do see more of
what I see around me.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:45.360 line:84%
I mean, I was born and
raised in Los Angeles,

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:49.150 line:84%
so diversity is not
a big thing for me.

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:51.880 line:84%
It was part of my
natural environment.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:53.610 line:90%
So yeah.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:58.170 line:84%
I think, as Rodrigo
says, it's about time.

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:01.920 line:84%
But I'm sure there's people
who are impatient and feel

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:04.500 line:84%
like the incremental changes
aren't happening fast enough.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:06.900 line:84%
But I see a big change
from when I first started

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:09.270 line:90%
in this business and to now.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:14.220 line:84%
I think television has been
a big instigator of change

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:18.330 line:84%
a lot more than movies, and they
started earlier than movies.

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:23.430 line:84%
And it's kind of almost a
mandate, in most television

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:27.360 line:84%
just in diversifying
their casts.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.130 line:90%
So they've taken the--

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:32.280 line:84%
they've taken this on and
really, really run with it.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:34.650 line:84%
I think, you know,
Elvis, I think especially

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:39.180 line:84%
in the case of directors,
there's often that knee jerk

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:42.320 line:84%
reaction, well, why can't
I do whatever I want to do?

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:44.640 line:84%
You know, why can't I use
the people I want to use?

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:48.670 line:84%
I mean, that's just
the kind of thing

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:50.920 line:84%
that comes with the DNA
of being a director.

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:54.880 line:84%
But again, I think
what I like about this,

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:56.860 line:84%
and I'm now speaking
as a filmmaker.

00:10:56.860 --> 00:11:00.370 line:84%
I'm not speaking as someone
who is on the Academy board

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:03.940 line:84%
and had somewhat
of a role in this.

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:07.150 line:84%
You know, these rules
are also an invitation

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:09.710 line:90%
for people to think differently.

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:11.920 line:84%
What if this
character was Chinese?

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:14.200 line:90%
What if this character was this?

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:16.430 line:90%
You know, it's a little bit--

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:20.200 line:84%
it sounds like medicine,
but it's an invitation

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:22.970 line:90%
to think in a different way.

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:24.670 line:84%
And I think that's
what can be valuable,

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:30.100 line:84%
and that's where directors
can satisfy what we're trying

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:34.390 line:84%
to do, but also satisfy
themselves that I'll do it,

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:36.750 line:84%
but it needs to be
a Guatemalan person.

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:40.326 line:84%
OK, well let it be
a Guatemalan person.

00:11:40.326 --> 00:11:42.640 line:84%
We have to find
that middle ground

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:46.060 line:84%
between the creative impulse
and the creative vision

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:50.590 line:84%
and what we're trying to do,
without as Vicky suggested,

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:53.490 line:84%
making things impossible
for filmmakers.

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:55.990 line:84%
I'm glad you brought that up
about directors kind of wanting

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:57.790 line:84%
their own way, because it'll
give me a chance to ask

00:11:57.790 --> 00:11:59.082 line:90%
Vicky and Julian this question.

00:11:59.082 --> 00:12:00.500 line:90%
You can pretend to not hear.

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:01.780 line:90%
Probably you'll answer this.

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:05.890 line:84%
But when you are talking
with filmmakers who do

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:09.370 line:84%
want things the way they want
them, and they aren't being--

00:12:12.203 --> 00:12:13.620 line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] they're
being stubborn

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:16.410 line:84%
about what they think they
want, but your job is really

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:20.040 line:84%
to expose them to ideas and to
actors they may not know about.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:21.666 line:90%
And does get to--

00:12:21.666 --> 00:12:23.900 line:84%
at what point did that get
to be easier from you guys

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:25.940 line:84%
as casting directors to
say, well, look at him,

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:27.360 line:84%
and not have
everyone go, well, I

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:30.240 line:84%
didn't see an Asian or a
Black person in that role.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:34.080 line:84%
When did that really start to be
a noticeable change in the way

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:35.490 line:90%
you guys work?

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:40.870 line:84%
I think in the
last several years.

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:45.270 line:84%
It used to be, I think,
a harder thing to change,

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:49.770 line:84%
I think, back in the 2000s,
or the '90s, or back then.

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:53.130 line:84%
But look, sometimes you
have to kind of come

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:57.620 line:84%
through a different, a
side door with directors,

00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:00.720 line:84%
if they weren't thinking about
a certain character being

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.780 line:84%
whatever, anything
different than what

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:06.810 line:90%
they had fixed in their minds.

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:09.880 line:84%
You just kind of try
to introduce someone,

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:12.120 line:84%
you know, after they've
seen several of the type

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:14.620 line:84%
that they're looking for, maybe
you kind of slip someone in,

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:16.800 line:84%
and it's a little bit
different, and they kind of

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.380 line:84%
get to hear it, and oh, OK,
I didn't think about that.

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:23.670 line:84%
Or just try to kind of go
in a back backdoor in a way,

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:28.350 line:84%
being respectful of what they
want, but also saying, here's

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:31.380 line:84%
another way we
could possibly go.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:34.230 line:84%
So it's really just kind
of finessing a little bit,

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:36.300 line:84%
kind of show them something
they might not have

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:38.680 line:90%
thought of before, or you know?

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:39.470 line:90%
Yeah.

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:41.640 line:84%
It's part of our job,
I think, to do that.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:47.062 line:84%
So we first read the script
and meet with the director,

00:13:47.062 --> 00:13:49.020 line:84%
if it's someone we've
never worked with before,

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:51.870 line:84%
and we hear their hopes
and dreams about it,

00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:54.510 line:84%
and get a good understanding
of what they're

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:55.860 line:90%
seeking for these roles.

00:13:55.860 --> 00:14:02.760 line:84%
But then, I think, trust in
our ability to bring some fresh

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:06.210 line:84%
ideas to the tables, and
out-of-the-box ideas.

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:09.300 line:84%
If they veto it,
you know, you tried.

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:13.410 line:84%
But I think it's our job
to expand their mind,

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:15.750 line:84%
because a lot of
directors I work with

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:17.430 line:84%
have written the
material as well,

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:19.200 line:84%
and you become
very married to how

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:21.300 line:90%
you've been thinking about it.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:23.160 line:84%
And I think that
casting directors

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.250 line:84%
are super instrumental
in broadening

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:28.590 line:84%
that sort of narrow
vision that they've

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:30.160 line:90%
been carrying this whole time.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:35.000 line:84%
And yeah, I think that's why we
like what we do, right Vicky?

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:37.080 line:84%
It can be difficult,
but as you said,

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:39.540 line:84%
Julia, they get very
married to something.

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:41.650 line:84%
I understand, it can
be a personal project.

00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:46.140 line:84%
It could be a script about
their life, or their family.

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:49.140 line:90%
And so you want to respect that.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:55.140 line:84%
And if-- and hopefully,
whatever different option

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:57.570 line:84%
you bring to them
helps accentuate

00:14:57.570 --> 00:15:03.053 line:84%
whatever they're trying to
communicate to an audience.

00:15:03.053 --> 00:15:04.470 line:84%
Well, I don't think
we're bringing

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:05.850 line:90%
in color for color's sake.

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:09.600 line:84%
I mean, I think like we
want the project to succeed,

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:13.110 line:84%
and we have the goodness of the
project and the success of it

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:19.520 line:84%
in mind with implementing some
of these out-of-the-box ideas.

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:22.000 line:90%
Well, go ahead, Rodrigo.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.320 line:84%
I was just actually thinking,
while Vicky and Julia were

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:27.820 line:84%
talking, that you know, I have
been in that situation, where

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:30.460 line:84%
I'm the writer,
director, and sometimes

00:15:30.460 --> 00:15:32.260 line:84%
the casting director
will say, well, how

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:35.100 line:84%
about Elvis Mitchell
for that role?

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:36.350 line:90%
And I'm like, no, I just--

00:15:36.350 --> 00:15:38.630 line:90%
I love him, but I don't see it.

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:42.290 line:84%
And you know, Elvis
Mitchell stayed on the list

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:44.930 line:84%
and stayed on the list,
and they brought him up,

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:46.430 line:90%
and they brought him up.

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:50.780 line:84%
And I remember a couple
cases in particular

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:54.440 line:84%
where one of the first
names I shot down,

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:56.870 line:84%
and three months
later, it was her.

00:15:56.870 --> 00:16:00.660 line:84%
And she was great
that it was her.

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:03.320 line:84%
And this was on a pilot,
not material I'd written.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:09.380 line:84%
But you know, that is where the
strengths of being a director

00:16:09.380 --> 00:16:13.700 line:84%
are so close to the weaknesses
of being a director.

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:15.990 line:90%
You know, the tunnel vision.

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:18.650 line:84%
And I think that's
where your heads

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:21.580 line:84%
of department, and of
course casting, is crucial.

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:23.330 line:90%
And yeah.

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:23.960 line:90%
I've seen that.

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:27.080 line:84%
Those names just stay on the
list and stay on the list,

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:31.730 line:84%
and one day you're
like, OK, bring him in.

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:33.230 line:84%
Just so you know,
I'm still waiting.

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:35.150 line:90%
But anyway.

00:16:35.150 --> 00:16:39.740 line:84%
I was going to ask you, do
you guys, Vicky and Julia,

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:43.390 line:84%
if it is that much
more difficult getting

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:45.778 line:84%
these kinds of changes
or these kind of ideas,

00:16:45.778 --> 00:16:47.570 line:84%
like you were saying,
for writer directors,

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:48.840 line:84%
so as you were
saying, Vicky, people

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:51.060 line:84%
who have lived with this
thing and dreamed about it,

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:53.050 line:84%
and been waiting
for years and years,

00:16:53.050 --> 00:16:56.240 line:84%
and it's like I voted
with these people in mind.

00:16:56.240 --> 00:17:03.050 line:84%
And sometimes, obviously,
you guys showed up to vote.

00:17:03.050 --> 00:17:06.180 line:84%
Well, that's a much more
interesting way to go.

00:17:06.180 --> 00:17:07.993 line:84%
But the writer
director way tends

00:17:07.993 --> 00:17:09.660 line:84%
to be that much harder
than just working

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:13.280 line:84%
with somebody who's not a
writer on the project, who's

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:14.510 line:90%
then directing.

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:15.740 line:90%
No, they can rewrite it.

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:17.162 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:21.710 line:84%
They can go, wait,
Eureka, you actually

00:17:21.710 --> 00:17:24.377 line:84%
opened my eyes to
something kind of different

00:17:24.377 --> 00:17:25.460 line:90%
that I didn't think about.

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:29.660 line:84%
Let me rework that character
to fit in this new introduction

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:31.710 line:84%
that you kind of
presented to me.

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:34.700 line:84%
So there is advantages to
working with writer directors

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:36.090 line:90%
too.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:39.290 line:84%
I think, you know, in some
ways casting directors

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:41.960 line:84%
are like editors in
the sense that they

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:48.440 line:84%
know their job is so much
about process, meaning time.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:52.730 line:84%
You cannot edit a movie
in a week with 17 editors.

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:55.660 line:90%
You need the 17 weeks.

00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:56.880 line:90%
And casting is the same.

00:17:56.880 --> 00:17:58.160 line:90%
You need to sleep on it.

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:01.250 line:84%
Whoever excites you Monday
doesn't excite you Thursday.

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:06.360 line:84%
And I think casting directors
have that incredible patience

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:10.140 line:84%
and knowledge that
time will tell.

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:12.817 line:90%
Yeah, very much so.

00:18:12.817 --> 00:18:14.400 line:84%
Oh, because it's
funny, because you're

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:19.080 line:84%
saying that, Vicky, about
certain kinds of projects

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:20.650 line:90%
demanding certain casts.

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:22.900 line:84%
I guess I just find
myself thinking,

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:25.570 line:84%
is this going to feel like
this new Best Picture thing,

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:29.120 line:84%
because I've heard this
phrase from hanging out

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:30.430 line:90%
with people so many times.

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:33.510 line:90%
I want to get behind it.

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:36.280 line:84%
People think it's the right
idea, and certainly an idea

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:39.370 line:90%
whose time has long since come.

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:42.890 line:84%
But also, just there
is the artistic ideal

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:47.550 line:90%
that you can't force chemistry.

00:18:47.550 --> 00:18:50.320 line:84%
And then people will
resist for reasons

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:55.320 line:84%
that, whether they be valid
or just kind of rationale

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:57.300 line:84%
because they want to
stick their ground,

00:18:57.300 --> 00:19:00.060 line:84%
I wonder about having those
kinds of conversations

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:02.730 line:84%
with directors and say, well,
at least be like this so you

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:04.710 line:90%
it can feel like this.

00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:09.240 line:84%
I mean, I found myself
being the only black person

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:12.330 line:84%
in the room arguing for
a character to be white,

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:16.250 line:84%
just because I think
because it makes

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:19.770 line:84%
kind of sense for a
certain power structure

00:19:19.770 --> 00:19:23.730 line:84%
that you're talking
about within the movie.

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:26.490 line:84%
And I know, I love to
push for diversity.

00:19:26.490 --> 00:19:28.330 line:90%
I'm all for it.

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:33.570 line:84%
But there are times when, yeah,
I was asking for the white guy,

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:37.530 line:90%
just because it really kind of--

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:42.120 line:84%
that casting, it's what the
project was talking about.

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:43.920 line:84%
It's the sort of
power imbalances

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:46.270 line:84%
that the project
was talking about.

00:19:46.270 --> 00:19:51.660 line:84%
So it just seemed to
make more sense that way.

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:53.560 line:84%
So I guess I've had that
sort of conversation

00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:56.940 line:84%
that you maybe wouldn't
think I would be having.

00:19:56.940 --> 00:19:59.040 line:84%
And it was noted
by the producer.

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:01.650 line:84%
I said, look, I just
think that's the right way

00:20:01.650 --> 00:20:03.530 line:90%
to go in this instance.

00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:04.030 line:90%
No, no.

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:05.730 line:84%
That's interesting,
because, I mean,

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:07.470 line:84%
I think one thing
is people of color,

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:08.887 line:84%
we bring to this
kind of thing, is

00:20:08.887 --> 00:20:12.027 line:84%
that we bring a point of
view that's not often had.

00:20:12.027 --> 00:20:14.610 line:84%
And I guess where we're getting,
and what you guys are talking

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:17.110 line:84%
about, and you're doing so well
here, is that point of view,

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:18.970 line:90%
and exercising it.

00:20:18.970 --> 00:20:24.928 line:84%
And I guess if that's something,
[INAUDIBLE] Rodrigo, if you're

00:20:24.928 --> 00:20:26.970 line:84%
having these conversations
about trying to create

00:20:26.970 --> 00:20:27.887 line:90%
a kind of a chemistry.

00:20:27.887 --> 00:20:31.510 line:84%
The directors, especially a
writer director, perhaps, I

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:33.630 line:84%
don't know, is really
wedded to the idea of it

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:37.380 line:84%
needs to be this, and you
would then just try to say,

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:39.230 line:84%
well, let's think
about this in terms

00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:41.820 line:84%
of the kind of social
fabric it creates.

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:46.830 line:84%
I don't cast a film for
social, like you know--

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:53.490 line:84%
I cast a film with the
success of the story,

00:20:53.490 --> 00:21:00.990 line:84%
and the meat of what is being
the director's intention

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:02.100 line:90%
at the heart.

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:06.510 line:84%
So I can't say that
I push for color when

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:08.700 line:84%
I'm feeling like
it's inappropriate

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:11.200 line:84%
or it's not serving
the characters well.

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:14.263 line:84%
Am I answering your
question correctly?

00:21:14.263 --> 00:21:15.680 line:84%
It's not-- no,
there's no correct.

00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:17.222 line:84%
But I guess, I just
wanted to assume,

00:21:17.222 --> 00:21:19.493 line:84%
because I think what
casting directors do,

00:21:19.493 --> 00:21:21.660 line:84%
and I think this is really
a chance that people hear

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:24.600 line:84%
about this, is offer,
again, a point of view,

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:30.135 line:84%
I think of as being as much
an artistic pursuit as costume

00:21:30.135 --> 00:21:31.260 line:90%
or makeup or anything else.

00:21:31.260 --> 00:21:33.900 line:84%
You guys have tried
to offer, as you were

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:36.510 line:90%
saying, supporting a vision.

00:21:36.510 --> 00:21:41.940 line:84%
But again, I guess kind
of arguing against a fixed

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:44.550 line:84%
point of view, and knowing how
hard that is for you to do,

00:21:44.550 --> 00:21:47.180 line:84%
and Vicky was talking
about the side door thing.

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:50.680 line:84%
Sometimes making a suggestion
that nobody was expecting.

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:54.170 line:84%
If you feel that kind of
freedom as often as not?

00:21:54.170 --> 00:21:59.270 line:84%
I mean, we have chemistry
reads sometimes to bring some,

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:03.050 line:84%
you know, to bring
some of those fears--

00:22:03.050 --> 00:22:06.360 line:90%
to sort of temper those.

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.720 line:84%
But if we don't have
access to actors

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:13.370 line:84%
that are going to actually come
in and do chemistry reads--

00:22:13.370 --> 00:22:15.918 line:84%
I don't know, I think
casting directors,

00:22:15.918 --> 00:22:17.960 line:84%
there's a certain leap of
faith and a sixth sense

00:22:17.960 --> 00:22:19.790 line:84%
that we have when
we feel something

00:22:19.790 --> 00:22:21.290 line:90%
is really going to work.

00:22:21.290 --> 00:22:24.650 line:84%
And I'm usually very
passionate about something

00:22:24.650 --> 00:22:25.970 line:90%
that I feel strongly about.

00:22:25.970 --> 00:22:27.900 line:84%
And I will fight
it till the end.

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:32.060 line:84%
And if I lose, then
you know, I tried.

00:22:32.060 --> 00:22:35.130 line:84%
But it is usually the director's
vision that I'm servicing.

00:22:35.130 --> 00:22:39.380 line:84%
So I know my place, and
I'm going to just serve up

00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:41.160 line:90%
my best options to him.

00:22:41.160 --> 00:22:43.800 line:90%
And ultimately, it's his choice.

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:46.590 line:84%
Although I do think
things can get very--

00:22:46.590 --> 00:22:53.025 line:84%
I think directors lives
when prepping a movie,

00:22:53.025 --> 00:22:54.900 line:84%
being in the middle of
preproduction, I mean,

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:55.920 line:90%
it's just very hectic.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:58.140 line:84%
There's a lot of
different questions

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:01.170 line:84%
coming at them, different
departments pulling on them.

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:05.700 line:84%
And I think it's
good to remind them

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:07.830 line:84%
of something that
can kind of get lost

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:12.120 line:84%
in that whole maelstrom
of preproduction,

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:16.380 line:84%
of having a million people
asking you questions.

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:19.950 line:84%
If we could just help
them keep their eye

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:26.110 line:84%
on the prize, so to speak,
like it can kind of be--

00:23:26.110 --> 00:23:30.360 line:84%
I remember Michael Mann
once said to me, he said--

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:31.710 line:90%
I asked him what it was.

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:34.500 line:84%
He says, I just want to
take a mental diuretic,

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:37.770 line:90%
because there's just so much--

00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:40.870 line:84%
just you know, he was
in the midst of it all.

00:23:40.870 --> 00:23:44.430 line:84%
And hopefully, on some level,
like every department head can

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:49.085 line:84%
kind of, just while
someone's about to,

00:23:49.085 --> 00:23:51.480 line:84%
you know, they're
against all these walls,

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:53.220 line:84%
that we can just
help remind them

00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:55.170 line:84%
of what they were
trying to achieve,

00:23:55.170 --> 00:23:58.690 line:84%
if it tends to go a
little bit off course,

00:23:58.690 --> 00:23:59.770 line:90%
if they need to remember.

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:01.290 line:84%
Now, remember, you
said you wanted

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:02.340 line:90%
this or that, or da, da, da.

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:04.923 line:84%
You know, just try to be there
to, like every other department

00:24:04.923 --> 00:24:10.650 line:84%
head, to help refocus
sometimes, and re-remind.

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:13.200 line:84%
And I think, you know,
in the case of casting,

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:15.150 line:90%
because you are casting--

00:24:15.150 --> 00:24:17.580 line:84%
you know, reading many
people for different roles,

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:21.330 line:84%
and like Vicky said, this
is all interlaced with all

00:24:21.330 --> 00:24:23.760 line:84%
the other things
that you're doing,

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:27.150 line:84%
you know, it's always good to
have a casting director to tell

00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:33.090 line:84%
you everyone you've cast in the
secondary professional roles is

00:24:33.090 --> 00:24:35.220 line:90%
a woman in her 30s.

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:41.010 line:84%
Or everyone you've cast
in the medical professions

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:42.150 line:90%
is a Latino.

00:24:42.150 --> 00:24:46.050 line:84%
So again, a reminder of
what the whole picture looks

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:48.900 line:90%
like, even beyond--

00:24:48.900 --> 00:24:51.780 line:84%
obviously, it's just
a reminder, does

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:57.480 line:84%
what we're doing here reflect
the world that the story takes

00:24:57.480 --> 00:24:59.280 line:84%
place in, the world
that you live in,

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:01.650 line:84%
the world that you
want to reflect?

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:05.110 line:84%
And that doesn't mean you have
to have a rainbow of colors.

00:25:05.110 --> 00:25:09.660 line:84%
But I think that's where the
casting director is crucial,

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:10.800 line:90%
giving you--

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:12.690 line:84%
reminding you of
the lay of the land.

00:25:12.690 --> 00:25:15.430 line:90%
This is where we are right now.

00:25:15.430 --> 00:25:16.330 line:90%
This is where we are.

00:25:16.330 --> 00:25:19.770 line:84%
This is what it looks
like, from an age, sex,

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:24.718 line:84%
sexual orientation, color, you
know, ethnicity, et cetera.

00:25:24.718 --> 00:25:26.010 line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] briefly a question.

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:28.530 line:84%
I'm going to start with you,
and ask [INAUDIBLE] these guys.

00:25:28.530 --> 00:25:30.197 line:84%
Talk to me about your
first conversation

00:25:30.197 --> 00:25:32.490 line:84%
as a casting director
on your first movie,

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:35.803 line:84%
and how your eyes were kind of
opened to exactly the things

00:25:35.803 --> 00:25:36.720 line:90%
they're talking about?

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:42.630 line:84%
Well, I am always
playing you first.

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:46.020 line:84%
People are eager to ask the
director for their idea,

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:48.270 line:90%
and I'm always to not give it.

00:25:48.270 --> 00:25:51.270 line:84%
I'm always like, you read
the script, you tell me.

00:25:51.270 --> 00:25:52.080 line:90%
What did you think?

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:54.810 line:84%
What did-- and I
want to hear that

00:25:54.810 --> 00:25:58.120 line:84%
from casting directors and
anyone in every department,

00:25:58.120 --> 00:25:59.820 line:90%
including the actors.

00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:01.920 line:84%
If an actor asks me,
what's your script about?

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:05.620 line:84%
I would say, what do
you think it's about?

00:26:05.620 --> 00:26:08.130 line:90%
You want-- here's the thing.

00:26:08.130 --> 00:26:11.970 line:84%
I feel that all departments
and all creative people,

00:26:11.970 --> 00:26:14.280 line:84%
they work from their
imagination, from their talent,

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:15.660 line:90%
from their experience.

00:26:15.660 --> 00:26:17.910 line:90%
I'm always reluctant to say--

00:26:17.910 --> 00:26:21.910 line:84%
sometimes a casting
director will say to me

00:26:21.910 --> 00:26:24.730 line:84%
right off the bat,
in the ideal world,

00:26:24.730 --> 00:26:28.600 line:84%
who would your ideal
three actors be?

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:32.133 line:84%
And I'm like, no, let
us surprise ourselves.

00:26:32.133 --> 00:26:33.550 line:84%
You tell me, you
know what I mean?

00:26:33.550 --> 00:26:38.340 line:84%
It's like, I'm reluctant
to say what I think.

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:40.850 line:84%
I'm reluctant to be
trapped in my own thing.

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:47.080 line:84%
And listen, I see it not just
with myself, but with directors

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:49.923 line:84%
who are friends, or
directors that I know,

00:26:49.923 --> 00:26:51.340 line:84%
and including some
of the greatest

00:26:51.340 --> 00:26:52.840 line:90%
directors in the world.

00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:56.230 line:84%
They had this idea, and then
in the end it wasn't the idea,

00:26:56.230 --> 00:26:59.620 line:84%
and they were thrilled
that it wasn't the idea.

00:26:59.620 --> 00:27:05.110 line:84%
So it's, again, it's what do you
think ends up being something

00:27:05.110 --> 00:27:09.340 line:84%
that as a director you should
be asking a lot, I think.

00:27:09.340 --> 00:27:11.502 line:90%
In my not so humble opinion.

00:27:11.502 --> 00:27:13.092 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:13.092 --> 00:27:14.800 line:84%
I like [INAUDIBLE] to
a lack of humility.

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:17.800 line:90%
That's always, always good.

00:27:17.800 --> 00:27:19.520 line:84%
[INAUDIBLE],, let
me ask you, Vicky,

00:27:19.520 --> 00:27:21.280 line:84%
do you remember
your first casting

00:27:21.280 --> 00:27:23.920 line:84%
job and what that
was like, and how

00:27:23.920 --> 00:27:27.180 line:84%
you were able to sort
of make breakthroughs,

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:32.350 line:84%
and kind of what's
that entail for you?

00:27:32.350 --> 00:27:38.220 line:84%
My first casting job
was on a student film

00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:39.560 line:90%
that became a feature film.

00:27:39.560 --> 00:27:42.130 line:84%
And so I didn't kind of go
through a traditional route.

00:27:42.130 --> 00:27:45.700 line:84%
I didn't go through working
for a casting director,

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:46.670 line:90%
being an assistant.

00:27:46.670 --> 00:27:49.043 line:84%
And so I kind--
and my first one,

00:27:49.043 --> 00:27:50.710 line:84%
I wouldn't really
consider kind of being

00:27:50.710 --> 00:27:52.630 line:90%
a part of the industry.

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:53.260 line:90%
It was just--

00:27:53.260 --> 00:27:55.720 line:84%
Hollywood, it was kind
of an independent film

00:27:55.720 --> 00:27:57.950 line:84%
that we all kind
of got together.

00:27:57.950 --> 00:27:58.850 line:90%
Let's make a movie.

00:27:58.850 --> 00:28:01.470 line:84%
And then somebody gave
us more money to make it.

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:03.070 line:90%
Oh, we're making a feature.

00:28:03.070 --> 00:28:09.070 line:84%
So it's kind of, just thrown
into the deep end of the pool,

00:28:09.070 --> 00:28:11.870 line:84%
and just learn to
swim, you know?

00:28:14.260 --> 00:28:14.760 line:90%
Yeah.

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:15.850 line:84%
So it didn't really
have anything

00:28:15.850 --> 00:28:17.440 line:84%
to do with the
business in a way.

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:20.470 line:84%
It kind of had to do
more with film school,

00:28:20.470 --> 00:28:24.370 line:84%
and an independent
group of people.

00:28:24.370 --> 00:28:25.870 line:84%
And I just kind of
went on instincts

00:28:25.870 --> 00:28:31.960 line:84%
I've had just watching tons
of movies and television shows

00:28:31.960 --> 00:28:32.740 line:90%
growing up.

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:36.330 line:84%
And kind of always
taking note of actors,

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:38.380 line:84%
and knowing which
actors I liked,

00:28:38.380 --> 00:28:44.680 line:84%
and not really planning
to kind of do this job.

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:48.430 line:84%
But once it got
thrown at me, it was

00:28:48.430 --> 00:28:52.990 line:84%
a skill set that I kind of had
and didn't quite know I had.

00:28:52.990 --> 00:29:00.800 line:84%
So I had a sense of actors very
early on that I saw growing up.

00:29:00.800 --> 00:29:03.720 line:84%
And I just kind of
referred to that

00:29:03.720 --> 00:29:08.542 line:84%
in my first casting meeting
and my first casting job.

00:29:08.542 --> 00:29:10.000 line:84%
In some ways, what
you're saying is

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:11.930 line:84%
you could have basically
had an affinity for this,

00:29:11.930 --> 00:29:14.138 line:84%
and you didn't realize it
until you started doing it.

00:29:14.138 --> 00:29:15.373 line:90%
Yes, I did.

00:29:15.373 --> 00:29:17.290 line:84%
And I actually had an
affinity for growing up.

00:29:17.290 --> 00:29:19.570 line:84%
I just wasn't being
a casting director.

00:29:19.570 --> 00:29:23.590 line:84%
I was being a movie addict
and a television addict,

00:29:23.590 --> 00:29:27.250 line:84%
and you know, loving all
the TV shows and the actors,

00:29:27.250 --> 00:29:28.750 line:84%
and I just had an
affinity for them.

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:31.540 line:84%
So yeah, I found out I
had an affinity for it.

00:29:31.540 --> 00:29:32.040 line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:34.052 line:84%
And Julie, what
was your first job,

00:29:34.052 --> 00:29:36.010 line:84%
like in your first
conversation with a director

00:29:36.010 --> 00:29:39.940 line:90%
about getting actors cast?

00:29:39.940 --> 00:29:43.110 line:84%
The one I can recall,
one of my early

00:29:43.110 --> 00:29:52.000 line:84%
like doing it on my own jobs,
it came with an actor that was

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:55.060 line:90%
somewhat established attached.

00:29:55.060 --> 00:29:57.430 line:90%
And that was thrilling.

00:29:57.430 --> 00:30:01.270 line:84%
The filmmaker had nurtured his
own relationship with an actor,

00:30:01.270 --> 00:30:04.150 line:84%
and an independent
film certainly

00:30:04.150 --> 00:30:05.870 line:90%
can use all the help it can get.

00:30:05.870 --> 00:30:09.700 line:84%
So to have an actor that
other actors respected--

00:30:09.700 --> 00:30:11.510 line:84%
I mean, he wasn't
a household name,

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:14.500 line:84%
but he was a working actor,
and he was doing good work.

00:30:14.500 --> 00:30:17.740 line:90%
And so it was Jeremy Sisto.

00:30:17.740 --> 00:30:22.960 line:84%
And then through being
able to promote and talk

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:26.050 line:84%
about the project with
him on board already,

00:30:26.050 --> 00:30:27.610 line:90%
it really helped--

00:30:27.610 --> 00:30:31.600 line:84%
I realized how much it
facilitated and attracted

00:30:31.600 --> 00:30:34.390 line:84%
other actors that wanted
to work with Jeremy.

00:30:34.390 --> 00:30:38.860 line:84%
And so, you know,
talking to agents

00:30:38.860 --> 00:30:42.730 line:84%
and having them tell you that
their client is just coming out

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:45.730 line:84%
in something that's
going to be huge,

00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:49.210 line:84%
and it would be such a coup
to have them in your film,

00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:52.150 line:84%
and just kind of
like seeing that it

00:30:52.150 --> 00:30:57.880 line:84%
was a little bit of a selling
relationship with the agent,

00:30:57.880 --> 00:31:00.850 line:84%
with the reps and us,
and being able to feel

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:04.300 line:84%
all the different
ideas being thrown,

00:31:04.300 --> 00:31:08.500 line:84%
it was a really exciting
and cool learning experience

00:31:08.500 --> 00:31:10.720 line:84%
to be able to put
the pieces together.

00:31:10.720 --> 00:31:14.110 line:84%
And also to have all these
names be thrown at you

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:17.710 line:84%
and to be able to say, you
know, they're not right.

00:31:17.710 --> 00:31:19.780 line:84%
So that was kind of
an interesting thing

00:31:19.780 --> 00:31:23.830 line:84%
to have these ideas
and talent options,

00:31:23.830 --> 00:31:28.000 line:84%
but to really service the story
and work with the director

00:31:28.000 --> 00:31:32.550 line:84%
and say, well, this big
name was pitched to us,

00:31:32.550 --> 00:31:34.440 line:90%
but I don't think they're right.

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:39.780 line:84%
So that was kind of a
interesting aspect to our job.

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:42.780 line:84%
How did it feel when you
first said that, Julia?

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:44.730 line:90%
The first time you said it.

00:31:44.730 --> 00:31:48.240 line:90%
It was actually like, I felt--

00:31:48.240 --> 00:31:50.340 line:84%
I mean, it's not
like a power thing,

00:31:50.340 --> 00:31:53.070 line:84%
but I think I felt
really like this

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:56.070 line:84%
is the job that I'm meant
to do because I really

00:31:56.070 --> 00:31:57.600 line:90%
love what I do.

00:31:57.600 --> 00:31:59.250 line:90%
And it's not about names.

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:00.720 line:90%
It's not about movie stars.

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:04.740 line:84%
It's about the story, and it's
about working collaboratively

00:32:04.740 --> 00:32:05.700 line:90%
with the director.

00:32:05.700 --> 00:32:07.390 line:90%
So it was good.

00:32:07.390 --> 00:32:08.712 line:90%
It was a good feeling.

00:32:08.712 --> 00:32:12.030 line:84%
So let me ask you something that
I want to ask everybody anyway,

00:32:12.030 --> 00:32:14.520 line:84%
and now is a good time
as any to be talking

00:32:14.520 --> 00:32:19.020 line:84%
about this, about the movies
or TV shows that make you think

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:21.420 line:84%
there's a place for
me in this world,

00:32:21.420 --> 00:32:23.930 line:84%
because that's kind of key,
and it's the sort of thing,

00:32:23.930 --> 00:32:28.860 line:84%
you're all talking about this
love affair for this medium,

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:30.840 line:90%
and this connection with talent.

00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:32.465 line:84%
And I just wondered
if there are things

00:32:32.465 --> 00:32:35.490 line:84%
that you saw as kids
that make you think,

00:32:35.490 --> 00:32:36.660 line:90%
I could be in this place.

00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:38.490 line:84%
I could do what these
people are doing.

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:42.392 line:84%
There will be some entree
for me here, even though

00:32:42.392 --> 00:32:44.850 line:84%
as people of color, obviously,
that wasn't always the case.

00:32:44.850 --> 00:32:47.430 line:84%
But you have to sort of
almost will it for yourself,

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:48.490 line:90%
for ourselves anyway.

00:32:48.490 --> 00:32:52.260 line:84%
I wonder what those examples
would be for you guys?

00:32:52.260 --> 00:32:54.580 line:84%
I mean, I grew up
in Mexico City.

00:32:54.580 --> 00:32:59.090 line:84%
So I did not feel that
sense of exclusion,

00:32:59.090 --> 00:33:04.820 line:84%
meaning there was a film
industry that it was quite big.

00:33:04.820 --> 00:33:10.900 line:84%
And then there were Hollywood
movies and American TV shows,

00:33:10.900 --> 00:33:12.770 line:84%
and that just seemed
so remote, you know?

00:33:12.770 --> 00:33:15.690 line:84%
I didn't even feel
excluded from it.

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:19.940 line:84%
It just-- that's like watching
movies from another planet.

00:33:19.940 --> 00:33:26.090 line:84%
But I came here to the American
Film Institute in the '80s,

00:33:26.090 --> 00:33:30.250 line:90%
and I was a cameraman back then.

00:33:30.250 --> 00:33:33.310 line:84%
And the idea of
Mexican cinematographer

00:33:33.310 --> 00:33:37.090 line:90%
was beyond exotic here.

00:33:37.090 --> 00:33:39.490 line:90%
It was almost eyebrow raising.

00:33:39.490 --> 00:33:44.140 line:84%
Although Guillermo Navarro was
already making his way slowly,

00:33:44.140 --> 00:33:45.070 line:90%
but well.

00:33:47.850 --> 00:33:51.570 line:84%
But yeah, that
was-- you know, I've

00:33:51.570 --> 00:33:54.630 line:84%
had the pleasure of seeing
how that has changed,

00:33:54.630 --> 00:33:57.420 line:84%
and how the day
came when you said,

00:33:57.420 --> 00:34:00.000 line:84%
oh, I need you to see the work
of a Mexican cinematographer,

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:01.850 line:90%
people would lean in.

00:34:01.850 --> 00:34:05.550 line:84%
After Navarro and Rodrigo
Prieto, Chivo Lubezki,

00:34:05.550 --> 00:34:07.440 line:90%
all those guys.

00:34:07.440 --> 00:34:09.239 line:84%
But luckily,
growing up, I didn't

00:34:09.239 --> 00:34:14.030 line:84%
feel that sense of exclusion
from beyond the industry

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:17.080 line:84%
that I-- in the country
where I was growing up.

00:34:17.080 --> 00:34:20.639 line:84%
But you said something, and
it's still something that you--

00:34:20.639 --> 00:34:22.749 line:84%
some ambtion that's
sort of stoked

00:34:22.749 --> 00:34:25.560 line:84%
in you seeing these movies
coming from this other planet

00:34:25.560 --> 00:34:27.929 line:84%
that you were talking about,
that seemed kind of remote,

00:34:27.929 --> 00:34:30.270 line:84%
and so I wondered what those
things would have been?

00:34:30.270 --> 00:34:35.010 line:84%
It felt very remote, but
only because it was far away

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:39.940 line:84%
and in another country, and not
because I felt it excluded me

00:34:39.940 --> 00:34:46.290 line:84%
as a result of my ethnicity,
culture, or color, I mean,

00:34:46.290 --> 00:34:47.999 line:84%
to be frank, because
you know, it's--

00:34:51.760 --> 00:34:54.989 line:90%
I wasn't in that world.

00:34:54.989 --> 00:34:57.850 line:84%
But yes, it was it was
extremely remote, honestly.

00:34:57.850 --> 00:35:00.930 line:84%
I don't think, you
know, for any of us--

00:35:00.930 --> 00:35:05.350 line:84%
and by us, I mean that
generation of Mexican directors

00:35:05.350 --> 00:35:07.330 line:84%
who's working here in
Hollywood, and which

00:35:07.330 --> 00:35:10.370 line:84%
includes some of the greatest
filmmakers of the moment.

00:35:10.370 --> 00:35:14.020 line:84%
You know, none of us really
thought we would even

00:35:14.020 --> 00:35:15.910 line:90%
have an international career.

00:35:15.910 --> 00:35:19.980 line:84%
The ambition was to
make movies in Mexico.

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:23.370 line:84%
So I guess there was a
psychological barrier,

00:35:23.370 --> 00:35:27.300 line:84%
that even if you're not
being told no you can't, we

00:35:27.300 --> 00:35:29.920 line:84%
were telling
ourselves it's going

00:35:29.920 --> 00:35:32.630 line:90%
to be really hard to get there.

00:35:32.630 --> 00:35:36.830 line:84%
So yes, I think, I'm
backpedaling already.

00:35:36.830 --> 00:35:41.660 line:84%
There was a psychology of
I could never get there,

00:35:41.660 --> 00:35:42.650 line:90%
you know?

00:35:42.650 --> 00:35:45.830 line:84%
But I'm sure young
people must have felt it

00:35:45.830 --> 00:35:51.890 line:84%
in St. Louis, Missouri, just
Hollywood seemed a planet away.

00:35:51.890 --> 00:35:53.540 line:84%
But also there were
things being beamed

00:35:53.540 --> 00:35:55.840 line:84%
from that planet, if I may
torture a metaphor here,

00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:58.730 line:84%
of movies or things that you
saw that you think, yeah,

00:35:58.730 --> 00:35:59.390 line:90%
I can do this.

00:35:59.390 --> 00:36:00.370 line:90%
There's a place for me.

00:36:00.370 --> 00:36:02.120 line:84%
I just wonder if, what
some of those were.

00:36:04.720 --> 00:36:09.190 line:84%
You know, I actually,
the neorealists,

00:36:09.190 --> 00:36:10.690 line:84%
you know, the
Italians, because they

00:36:10.690 --> 00:36:15.070 line:84%
were telling stories of working
people, middle class people.

00:36:15.070 --> 00:36:17.890 line:84%
The actors were not
necessarily beautiful.

00:36:17.890 --> 00:36:19.420 line:90%
They were not Hollywood people.

00:36:19.420 --> 00:36:21.460 line:90%
A lot of them were non-actors.

00:36:21.460 --> 00:36:29.200 line:84%
So I would say that watching all
those Italian neorealist movies

00:36:29.200 --> 00:36:31.270 line:90%
made you think, oh, wow, OK.

00:36:31.270 --> 00:36:34.090 line:84%
Well, that looks
like people that

00:36:34.090 --> 00:36:37.300 line:84%
could be like us, like people
from Mexico City, middle class,

00:36:37.300 --> 00:36:38.560 line:90%
working class.

00:36:38.560 --> 00:36:41.140 line:84%
I think that's the closest
thing to say, oh, OK.

00:36:41.140 --> 00:36:42.700 line:84%
It doesn't all have
to be Hollywood.

00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:43.970 line:90%
It could be this.

00:36:43.970 --> 00:36:46.630 line:84%
And is there-- were movies like
that for you as well, Julia,

00:36:46.630 --> 00:36:48.838 line:84%
or things that make you
think, well, I could do this,

00:36:48.838 --> 00:36:51.700 line:90%
or there's a place for me here?

00:36:51.700 --> 00:36:53.620 line:84%
Well, born and
raised in Los Angeles

00:36:53.620 --> 00:37:00.370 line:84%
does not mean it's an automatic
in to the town's industry.

00:37:00.370 --> 00:37:03.870 line:90%
I did feel--

00:37:03.870 --> 00:37:06.240 line:84%
I mean, it wasn't
uncommon for me

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:10.020 line:84%
to be the only person
of color in an office.

00:37:10.020 --> 00:37:11.370 line:90%
But I did feel--

00:37:11.370 --> 00:37:14.460 line:84%
I never felt hyper
aware of that.

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:17.430 line:84%
I did feel a sense
of belonging, and I

00:37:17.430 --> 00:37:22.770 line:84%
gravitated towards like weird
movies, like David Lynch

00:37:22.770 --> 00:37:24.990 line:90%
and just--

00:37:24.990 --> 00:37:31.560 line:84%
I liked that there were voices
that were not exactly making

00:37:31.560 --> 00:37:33.600 line:84%
sense, and they were
just kind of marching

00:37:33.600 --> 00:37:34.590 line:90%
to their own drummer.

00:37:34.590 --> 00:37:38.070 line:84%
And so I was like, oh,
I'll join that club please.

00:37:38.070 --> 00:37:40.800 line:84%
I feel like I can work
with people like that.

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:43.080 line:90%
So yeah.

00:37:43.080 --> 00:37:48.500 line:84%
I think I definitely felt
like there was a place for me.

00:37:48.500 --> 00:37:50.660 line:84%
And those are the kind of
filmmakers you are still

00:37:50.660 --> 00:37:52.912 line:84%
working with, people who
hear those weird voices

00:37:52.912 --> 00:37:55.370 line:84%
in their head, that they want
to get on that screen, right?

00:37:55.370 --> 00:37:56.510 line:90%
Yeah, that'll never change.

00:37:56.510 --> 00:37:57.140 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:37:57.140 --> 00:37:57.650 line:90%
For sure.

00:38:00.273 --> 00:38:01.190 line:90%
What about you, Vicky?

00:38:01.190 --> 00:38:02.898 line:84%
What what are some of
the things that you

00:38:02.898 --> 00:38:05.260 line:84%
saw being such a consumer
of this kind of stuff?

00:38:05.260 --> 00:38:08.010 line:84%
What made you think, well,
God, I could be in this.

00:38:08.010 --> 00:38:09.420 line:90%
I could do this kind of thing.

00:38:09.420 --> 00:38:11.990 line:84%
Because I think, I just want
to get this out to young people

00:38:11.990 --> 00:38:14.960 line:84%
watching this who do have
these kinds of questions,

00:38:14.960 --> 00:38:18.080 line:84%
or even growing up, as you
said, living in Los Angeles,

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:20.090 line:84%
you still, if you grew
up here, doesn't mean

00:38:20.090 --> 00:38:21.620 line:90%
you're in the movie business.

00:38:21.620 --> 00:38:23.120 line:84%
So Vicky, I'm just
wondering what

00:38:23.120 --> 00:38:25.430 line:90%
those things would be for you.

00:38:25.430 --> 00:38:28.070 line:90%
You know, I grew up in LA too.

00:38:28.070 --> 00:38:30.080 line:84%
My dad worked at
the post office.

00:38:30.080 --> 00:38:31.880 line:90%
My mom sold encyclopedia.

00:38:31.880 --> 00:38:34.550 line:84%
We were nowhere near
Hollywood in terms

00:38:34.550 --> 00:38:37.580 line:90%
of being near that profession.

00:38:37.580 --> 00:38:41.420 line:90%
But I just always, I never--

00:38:41.420 --> 00:38:45.260 line:84%
I mean, everything I saw told
me I could work in the industry.

00:38:45.260 --> 00:38:47.900 line:84%
I didn't say anything
that told me I couldn't.

00:38:47.900 --> 00:38:49.712 line:90%
I mean, I just--

00:38:49.712 --> 00:38:57.230 line:84%
and I looked at every type of
movie, all types of TV shows.

00:38:57.230 --> 00:38:59.450 line:84%
I think, mainly my
parents never told us

00:38:59.450 --> 00:39:01.610 line:90%
we couldn't not do something.

00:39:01.610 --> 00:39:09.270 line:84%
So for me, wanting to be in show
business seemed to make sense.

00:39:09.270 --> 00:39:14.330 line:84%
And I didn't really
look at any obstacles.

00:39:14.330 --> 00:39:16.190 line:84%
Because I'm asking
this just because you

00:39:16.190 --> 00:39:18.080 line:84%
guys are all
extraordinarily successful.

00:39:18.080 --> 00:39:21.810 line:84%
And again, I think what's
so interesting about you,

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:24.890 line:84%
you all have very
specific points of view.

00:39:24.890 --> 00:39:29.000 line:84%
And being in a business
that is often as not,

00:39:29.000 --> 00:39:31.000 line:84%
doesn't seem to
welcome points of view,

00:39:31.000 --> 00:39:34.820 line:84%
just the position to be
filled, what it's like for you

00:39:34.820 --> 00:39:38.090 line:84%
to continue to try to work
as artists in the field?

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:39.790 line:90%
That's the fun part.

00:39:39.790 --> 00:39:41.300 line:90%
I mean, you know, yeah.

00:39:41.300 --> 00:39:44.600 line:84%
I mean, I'd love to bring
a point of view to it,

00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:47.990 line:90%
but I don't know that--

00:39:47.990 --> 00:39:51.800 line:84%
even with something
that's typically

00:39:51.800 --> 00:39:55.190 line:84%
Hollywood, a big budget
Hollywood blockbuster,

00:39:55.190 --> 00:39:59.120 line:84%
hopefully you can infuse
something with the casting

00:39:59.120 --> 00:40:03.530 line:84%
or whatever that kind of
helps give it a little tweak

00:40:03.530 --> 00:40:06.560 line:84%
and make it a little
more interesting than a,

00:40:06.560 --> 00:40:10.640 line:84%
let's say a normal Hollywood
big blockbuster movie.

00:40:10.640 --> 00:40:13.670 line:84%
I'm just using something, those
are kind of generic terms.

00:40:13.670 --> 00:40:16.220 line:84%
But yeah, hopefully just
bring a point of view

00:40:16.220 --> 00:40:18.920 line:90%
to whatever you're working on.

00:40:18.920 --> 00:40:24.600 line:84%
And hopefully there's room in
it to bring your point of view.

00:40:24.600 --> 00:40:28.760 line:84%
Sometimes there's not, and
you kind of-- you kind of have

00:40:28.760 --> 00:40:30.610 line:90%
to, OK, this is what--

00:40:30.610 --> 00:40:32.180 line:90%
say this is what this is.

00:40:32.180 --> 00:40:36.290 line:84%
And OK-- you just kind of
take a step back a little bit.

00:40:36.290 --> 00:40:39.410 line:84%
You fight the fight
as much as you can.

00:40:39.410 --> 00:40:42.050 line:84%
And sometimes you don't
always win those fights,

00:40:42.050 --> 00:40:44.100 line:90%
or people don't always want to--

00:40:44.100 --> 00:40:49.180 line:84%
they just want to kind of make
the generic product in a way,

00:40:49.180 --> 00:40:50.090 line:90%
you know?

00:40:50.090 --> 00:40:54.020 line:84%
So sometimes your point of
view is appropriate to things,

00:40:54.020 --> 00:40:57.780 line:84%
and it may be less
appropriate to other things,

00:40:57.780 --> 00:40:59.750 line:90%
if that makes any sense?

00:40:59.750 --> 00:41:00.250 line:90%
No, it does.

00:41:00.250 --> 00:41:01.417 line:90%
It is what I was asking you.

00:41:01.417 --> 00:41:02.340 line:90%
Yeah, thank you.

00:41:02.340 --> 00:41:03.490 line:90%
I wonder if that--

00:41:03.490 --> 00:41:04.990 line:84%
has that been the
case for you ever,

00:41:04.990 --> 00:41:08.240 line:84%
Rodrigo, when you
felt that [INAUDIBLE]..

00:41:08.240 --> 00:41:12.320 line:84%
You know, I always say to
younger writer directors,

00:41:12.320 --> 00:41:14.780 line:84%
or writers, or
directors, you know,

00:41:14.780 --> 00:41:17.100 line:84%
if you have some success
when you're starting,

00:41:17.100 --> 00:41:19.310 line:84%
you have to be careful
that you're not

00:41:19.310 --> 00:41:23.270 line:84%
being paid not to be an
artist, because you do

00:41:23.270 --> 00:41:24.590 line:90%
get paid not to be an artist.

00:41:24.590 --> 00:41:30.050 line:84%
Sometimes if you're a young
up and coming screenwriter,

00:41:30.050 --> 00:41:34.970 line:84%
they'll pay you a lot of money
to rewrite a script in a way

00:41:34.970 --> 00:41:38.810 line:84%
that it's safe and
really not great.

00:41:38.810 --> 00:41:40.500 line:84%
But that's what
you're being paid for.

00:41:40.500 --> 00:41:42.200 line:84%
You're being paid
not to be an artist.

00:41:42.200 --> 00:41:46.610 line:84%
Don't bring your dreams, your
nightmares, your sickness

00:41:46.610 --> 00:41:48.020 line:90%
into a project.

00:41:48.020 --> 00:41:49.850 line:90%
Just write me a pretty picture.

00:41:49.850 --> 00:41:54.710 line:84%
So yes, you have to be
careful that you're not

00:41:54.710 --> 00:41:55.730 line:90%
leaning into that.

00:41:55.730 --> 00:41:57.380 line:84%
And you know, yeah,
to Vicky's point,

00:41:57.380 --> 00:41:58.730 line:90%
you have to voice your opinion.

00:41:58.730 --> 00:42:00.380 line:84%
You're also hired
for your opinion.

00:42:00.380 --> 00:42:02.880 line:84%
They may or may not
pay attention to it.

00:42:02.880 --> 00:42:05.550 line:90%
But you have to do it.

00:42:05.550 --> 00:42:07.520 line:84%
And I think in
some cases, that's

00:42:07.520 --> 00:42:13.940 line:84%
why often directors become
famous for being difficult,

00:42:13.940 --> 00:42:18.050 line:84%
and certainly some heads of
department, and of course,

00:42:18.050 --> 00:42:21.050 line:84%
it's very commonly said with
women heads of department,

00:42:21.050 --> 00:42:24.170 line:84%
she's difficult. No,
she's not difficult.

00:42:24.170 --> 00:42:26.540 line:84%
She's telling you what
she thinks because that's

00:42:26.540 --> 00:42:28.820 line:90%
what you hired her for.

00:42:28.820 --> 00:42:33.320 line:84%
So that's-- but you know, that's
a problem in this industry

00:42:33.320 --> 00:42:34.880 line:90%
and in many industries.

00:42:34.880 --> 00:42:40.220 line:84%
Do you want to speak up or do
you just want to get along?

00:42:40.220 --> 00:42:45.150 line:84%
Yeah, I mean, I think,
there are directors

00:42:45.150 --> 00:42:49.210 line:84%
who have never compromised,
like David Lynch, for example,

00:42:49.210 --> 00:42:50.910 line:90%
and all the power to him.

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:54.630 line:84%
But he, like Orson Welles
and many other greats,

00:42:54.630 --> 00:42:57.870 line:84%
found it increasingly
difficult to make movies,

00:42:57.870 --> 00:43:00.390 line:90%
because they did not compromise.

00:43:00.390 --> 00:43:05.160 line:90%
So it's also a life choice.

00:43:05.160 --> 00:43:06.480 line:90%
What about for you, Julia?

00:43:06.480 --> 00:43:08.615 line:84%
Have you been in
situations where it's--

00:43:08.615 --> 00:43:10.740 line:84%
you're trying to fight
against the idea of it being

00:43:10.740 --> 00:43:14.040 line:84%
a product when you see
something more in the material,

00:43:14.040 --> 00:43:16.890 line:84%
and I just wonder how
you deal with that?

00:43:16.890 --> 00:43:22.920 line:84%
I've been fortunate to have very
minimal experience with that.

00:43:22.920 --> 00:43:25.740 line:84%
When I meet-- when a
material is presented to me

00:43:25.740 --> 00:43:29.010 line:84%
and I read a script,
I spark to the ones

00:43:29.010 --> 00:43:33.660 line:84%
that I can see myself
contributing something artistic

00:43:33.660 --> 00:43:34.170 line:90%
to it.

00:43:37.040 --> 00:43:41.060 line:84%
I gravitate towards
something that's unique,

00:43:41.060 --> 00:43:43.640 line:84%
and I gravitate
towards directors

00:43:43.640 --> 00:43:45.560 line:84%
that have a collaborative
spirit that are

00:43:45.560 --> 00:43:49.290 line:90%
open to me saying what I feel.

00:43:49.290 --> 00:43:53.510 line:84%
And I haven't had
a lot of experience

00:43:53.510 --> 00:43:55.900 line:90%
where I had to fight.

00:43:55.900 --> 00:44:03.560 line:84%
I've, like I said, I haven't
won every battle of a casting

00:44:03.560 --> 00:44:04.760 line:90%
choice that I've presented.

00:44:04.760 --> 00:44:07.460 line:90%
But I've had a lot of--

00:44:07.460 --> 00:44:09.470 line:84%
I've had a lot of good
fortune with the projects

00:44:09.470 --> 00:44:10.310 line:90%
that I've worked on.

00:44:10.310 --> 00:44:13.940 line:90%
And it's someone intentional.

00:44:13.940 --> 00:44:16.160 line:84%
I have to love the
material and feel

00:44:16.160 --> 00:44:20.910 line:84%
like there's something I can add
to what's already on the page.

00:44:20.910 --> 00:44:24.620 line:84%
Was that the case with The Last
Black Man In San Francisco?

00:44:24.620 --> 00:44:25.910 line:90%
Absolutely.

00:44:25.910 --> 00:44:27.200 line:90%
Yes.

00:44:27.200 --> 00:44:29.780 line:90%
It's unique.

00:44:29.780 --> 00:44:32.520 line:90%
It grabbed me from the start.

00:44:32.520 --> 00:44:36.295 line:84%
The people behind it,
I could tell it just,

00:44:36.295 --> 00:44:37.670 line:84%
they were so
passionate about it,

00:44:37.670 --> 00:44:39.140 line:90%
and it was super infectious.

00:44:39.140 --> 00:44:43.430 line:84%
And I just really wanted
to fulfill their vision

00:44:43.430 --> 00:44:47.770 line:90%
to make them happy, for sure.

00:44:47.770 --> 00:44:49.530 line:84%
In a situation like
that, it's been,

00:44:49.530 --> 00:44:52.220 line:84%
we're talking about
for me, things

00:44:52.220 --> 00:44:53.990 line:84%
you guys have in
common is you guys

00:44:53.990 --> 00:44:56.540 line:84%
tend to be really good
at creating communities.

00:44:56.540 --> 00:44:58.670 line:84%
And that San
Francisco, that movie

00:44:58.670 --> 00:45:01.580 line:84%
felt like a real place and
not the real San Francisco,

00:45:01.580 --> 00:45:02.080 line:90%
if you will.

00:45:02.080 --> 00:45:03.830 line:90%
It felt like its own thing.

00:45:03.830 --> 00:45:08.750 line:84%
And I think that idea of
creating a world that feels

00:45:08.750 --> 00:45:13.370 line:84%
lived in but distinctive
is what you want to do.

00:45:13.370 --> 00:45:13.880 line:90%
Yeah.

00:45:13.880 --> 00:45:17.210 line:90%
I really credit those guys.

00:45:17.210 --> 00:45:21.110 line:84%
There's really not
an inauthentic beat.

00:45:21.110 --> 00:45:24.740 line:84%
You know, maybe there
had to be for some--

00:45:24.740 --> 00:45:28.460 line:84%
like Danny Glover even,
he's a commercial actor,

00:45:28.460 --> 00:45:31.190 line:84%
and sometimes I'm
not always keen

00:45:31.190 --> 00:45:34.310 line:84%
on having so someone well known
play a character, because it

00:45:34.310 --> 00:45:36.740 line:84%
might take you out of
the story, and then

00:45:36.740 --> 00:45:39.730 line:84%
the authenticness
of the story is--

00:45:39.730 --> 00:45:41.840 line:90%
it suffers a little bit.

00:45:41.840 --> 00:45:44.180 line:90%
But he's from the Bay Area.

00:45:44.180 --> 00:45:49.250 line:84%
He is a beloved person
from the communities

00:45:49.250 --> 00:45:51.200 line:90%
that the story is about.

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:54.020 line:84%
He had deep knowledge of
what the story was about.

00:45:54.020 --> 00:45:56.510 line:84%
And he really sparked
to it as well,

00:45:56.510 --> 00:46:01.727 line:84%
so it felt like a logical
marriage for sure.

00:46:01.727 --> 00:46:04.060 line:84%
And for you, Vicky, your first
conversation with Quentin

00:46:04.060 --> 00:46:05.477 line:84%
Tarantino, you
guys must have felt

00:46:05.477 --> 00:46:07.300 line:84%
you've know each other
your entire lives.

00:46:07.300 --> 00:46:08.150 line:90%
Yes.

00:46:08.150 --> 00:46:09.070 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:46:09.070 --> 00:46:12.340 line:84%
I think we were, like we grew
up in the same household, just

00:46:12.340 --> 00:46:17.020 line:84%
like 20 miles away, you know,
South Bay and and South LA.

00:46:17.020 --> 00:46:21.250 line:84%
Yeah, it was kind of, yeah,
brother from another mother.

00:46:21.250 --> 00:46:23.710 line:90%
It was, yeah.

00:46:23.710 --> 00:46:28.990 line:84%
We were the two same
nerdy kids growing up.

00:46:28.990 --> 00:46:34.030 line:84%
So I felt an
affinity, definitely.

00:46:34.030 --> 00:46:35.770 line:84%
There's also the
thing, too, again,

00:46:35.770 --> 00:46:38.110 line:84%
talking about creating
real communities.

00:46:38.110 --> 00:46:41.180 line:84%
What does that feel even
like [INAUDIBLE] behind you?

00:46:41.180 --> 00:46:43.150 line:84%
You've got to build
out something, well,

00:46:43.150 --> 00:46:46.570 line:84%
Once Upon A Time In
Hollywood, that feels bigger

00:46:46.570 --> 00:46:50.050 line:90%
than life, but not [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:50.050 --> 00:46:52.360 line:84%
And that must have
been sort of the thrill

00:46:52.360 --> 00:46:54.820 line:90%
and the challenge of that.

00:46:54.820 --> 00:46:58.570 line:84%
Yeah, I mean, I grew up in LA,
going to Hollywood Boulevard,

00:46:58.570 --> 00:46:59.410 line:90%
taking the bus.

00:46:59.410 --> 00:47:02.360 line:84%
Or my dad would drop
me and my friends off.

00:47:02.360 --> 00:47:04.690 line:84%
And I remember the events
of the movie happening.

00:47:07.970 --> 00:47:10.720 line:84%
It's just one I wanted to really
get right, kind of you know.

00:47:10.720 --> 00:47:13.990 line:84%
And yeah, it's
kind of heightened

00:47:13.990 --> 00:47:16.460 line:84%
but very realistic
at the same time.

00:47:16.460 --> 00:47:23.410 line:90%
And yeah, it was just a--

00:47:23.410 --> 00:47:25.900 line:90%
yeah, that was special one.

00:47:25.900 --> 00:47:30.040 line:84%
I have a girlfriend that worked
with him at the video store

00:47:30.040 --> 00:47:32.950 line:90%
that he worked at.

00:47:32.950 --> 00:47:34.330 line:90%
He knew every title.

00:47:34.330 --> 00:47:35.560 line:90%
He knew every shelf.

00:47:35.560 --> 00:47:37.480 line:90%
That's what she told me.

00:47:37.480 --> 00:47:39.410 line:90%
Yeah.

00:47:39.410 --> 00:47:41.990 line:84%
But I mean, this is so, and
this is for you too, Rodrigo.

00:47:41.990 --> 00:47:45.040 line:84%
I mean, this idea of
creating something that feels

00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:47.210 line:90%
real and bigger than life.

00:47:47.210 --> 00:47:51.070 line:84%
Because so often movies tend
to be these divided things,

00:47:51.070 --> 00:47:53.120 line:90%
they're either one or the other.

00:47:53.120 --> 00:47:55.220 line:84%
And I think that
kind of specificity

00:47:55.220 --> 00:47:58.090 line:84%
that that kind of
creation demands

00:47:58.090 --> 00:48:00.400 line:84%
is something you saw
in those neorealists,

00:48:00.400 --> 00:48:03.010 line:84%
and something that you want to
bring to the screen as well,

00:48:03.010 --> 00:48:04.553 line:90%
isn't it?

00:48:04.553 --> 00:48:05.970 line:84%
Yeah, I mean,
especially if you're

00:48:05.970 --> 00:48:12.750 line:84%
making contemporary, realistic,
psychological dramas,

00:48:12.750 --> 00:48:16.860 line:84%
how do you give it some
pop without it turning

00:48:16.860 --> 00:48:19.350 line:90%
into something that it's not?

00:48:19.350 --> 00:48:21.180 line:90%
Not too flat, not too poppy.

00:48:21.180 --> 00:48:27.200 line:84%
I mean, I'd say a
movie like, is it

00:48:27.200 --> 00:48:29.270 line:84%
The Farewell, the one with
the dying grandmother?

00:48:29.270 --> 00:48:30.440 line:90%
I don't want to give-- yes.

00:48:30.440 --> 00:48:35.990 line:84%
And that was, I thought that was
a very particular world, a very

00:48:35.990 --> 00:48:39.160 line:84%
particular tone,
and yet really just

00:48:39.160 --> 00:48:44.780 line:84%
a middle class family
Chinese, Chinese-Americans.

00:48:44.780 --> 00:48:48.470 line:84%
But it managed to
do that, create

00:48:48.470 --> 00:48:54.760 line:84%
that sort of slightly
heightened but totally grounded.

00:48:54.760 --> 00:48:57.760 line:90%
I think casting can do that.

00:48:57.760 --> 00:49:01.900 line:84%
It's always tricky
because like Julia said,

00:49:01.900 --> 00:49:05.890 line:84%
it's you're always scared,
oh, can I mix this star

00:49:05.890 --> 00:49:07.960 line:90%
with these mortals?

00:49:07.960 --> 00:49:10.540 line:84%
Is that going to be-- is
there going to be glue?

00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:12.550 line:84%
But there's also
a reason why stars

00:49:12.550 --> 00:49:15.640 line:84%
are stars, is because
they are great actors

00:49:15.640 --> 00:49:17.740 line:84%
and they fit in
beautifully into movies.

00:49:17.740 --> 00:49:20.540 line:84%
I mean, I've been
in that situation,

00:49:20.540 --> 00:49:23.170 line:84%
fortunately, where
I'm like, no, no, I

00:49:23.170 --> 00:49:26.740 line:84%
can't mix these two
new people with this--

00:49:26.740 --> 00:49:27.640 line:90%
but it works.

00:49:30.165 --> 00:49:32.290 line:84%
You're trying to achieve
that, something heightened

00:49:32.290 --> 00:49:37.300 line:84%
and special without your casting
being artificial and forced.

00:49:37.300 --> 00:49:39.850 line:84%
I think that's a
constant battle, I think.

00:49:39.850 --> 00:49:44.290 line:84%
I think that's a constant,
whether you're mixing lesser

00:49:44.290 --> 00:49:46.570 line:90%
knowns and well-known people.

00:49:46.570 --> 00:49:51.640 line:84%
Just you're always-- you
want to walk that fine line.

00:49:51.640 --> 00:49:54.440 line:84%
And I don't think that ever goes
away for a casting director,

00:49:54.440 --> 00:49:56.620 line:84%
no matter how long
you cast, you know?

00:49:58.713 --> 00:50:00.880 line:84%
You're always just trying
to get that balance right,

00:50:00.880 --> 00:50:05.290 line:84%
and just making sure that
you're keeping something real,

00:50:05.290 --> 00:50:07.060 line:84%
and as Julia said,
will the addition

00:50:07.060 --> 00:50:10.960 line:84%
of that big name in this small
part do this, or I don't know.

00:50:10.960 --> 00:50:14.650 line:84%
I've been constantly
surprised when, no, it

00:50:14.650 --> 00:50:16.000 line:90%
doesn't all the time.

00:50:16.000 --> 00:50:18.150 line:84%
It's just-- but the
tensions are always there.

00:50:18.150 --> 00:50:19.600 line:90%
I always get a little nervous.

00:50:19.600 --> 00:50:21.322 line:84%
I think it's a little
nerve wracking,

00:50:21.322 --> 00:50:22.780 line:84%
I think, for all
casting directors.

00:50:22.780 --> 00:50:25.320 line:84%
No matter how long
you've been doing it.

00:50:25.320 --> 00:50:28.960 line:84%
Is that the fun of it that's
a little anxiety producing?

00:50:28.960 --> 00:50:30.280 line:90%
It's definitely anxiety--

00:50:30.280 --> 00:50:33.040 line:84%
I mean, it's anxiety
producing, and I mean,

00:50:33.040 --> 00:50:34.820 line:84%
it's almost like
you ask yourself,

00:50:34.820 --> 00:50:36.355 line:84%
did I just start
casting yesterday,

00:50:36.355 --> 00:50:39.648 line:84%
because how come I don't
feel better about this?

00:50:39.648 --> 00:50:40.690 line:90%
Or how come I don't know?

00:50:40.690 --> 00:50:42.290 line:90%
That's the thing.

00:50:42.290 --> 00:50:44.260 line:90%
Don't you know this by now?

00:50:44.260 --> 00:50:47.520 line:90%
You know, it's yeah.

00:50:47.520 --> 00:50:50.630 line:84%
Well, you did it beautifully in
Once Upon a Time In Hollywood.

00:50:50.630 --> 00:50:53.260 line:90%
That was a big order to fill.

00:50:53.260 --> 00:50:55.390 line:90%
And I got lost in this story.

00:50:55.390 --> 00:50:57.200 line:90%
So kudos to you guys.

00:50:57.200 --> 00:50:57.700 line:90%
Thank you.

00:50:57.700 --> 00:50:58.470 line:90%
Kudos to you.

00:50:58.470 --> 00:51:01.540 line:84%
And I think part
of that that fear

00:51:01.540 --> 00:51:04.990 line:84%
that Vicky is talking about, you
never recuperate from a casting

00:51:04.990 --> 00:51:05.780 line:90%
mistake.

00:51:05.780 --> 00:51:09.070 line:84%
I mean, it really is a knife
in your side forever and ever.

00:51:09.070 --> 00:51:11.050 line:84%
And it has nothing-- and
it doesn't necessarily

00:51:11.050 --> 00:51:14.740 line:84%
have anything to do with casting
stars and non-stars, actors

00:51:14.740 --> 00:51:15.970 line:90%
and non-actors.

00:51:15.970 --> 00:51:21.400 line:84%
Because you can have
the right level of fame

00:51:21.400 --> 00:51:23.570 line:84%
but still cast the
role that you're like,

00:51:23.570 --> 00:51:28.040 line:84%
oh, no, that was the wrong
tone, or she was too young,

00:51:28.040 --> 00:51:30.590 line:90%
he was too old.

00:51:30.590 --> 00:51:32.050 line:84%
You know, casting
can be traumatic,

00:51:32.050 --> 00:51:34.480 line:84%
because once you
cast the role, it's

00:51:34.480 --> 00:51:36.820 line:84%
going to be that face,
that body, that voice,

00:51:36.820 --> 00:51:40.000 line:84%
that instrument
forever and ever.

00:51:40.000 --> 00:51:44.290 line:84%
So it can be preoccupying,
to put it mildly.

00:51:44.290 --> 00:51:45.900 line:90%
It's a heavy burden.

00:51:45.900 --> 00:51:46.780 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:51:46.780 --> 00:51:47.605 line:90%
I have no doubt.

00:51:47.605 --> 00:51:49.480 line:84%
You make me so anxious
talking about it here,

00:51:49.480 --> 00:51:50.500 line:90%
and I don't even do it.

00:51:50.500 --> 00:51:53.290 line:84%
But I guess, as we're
talking about this too,

00:51:53.290 --> 00:51:56.620 line:84%
this made me go back to thinking
about the most recent USC

00:51:56.620 --> 00:51:58.540 line:84%
Annenberg study about
inclusion, again.

00:51:58.540 --> 00:52:01.630 line:84%
We're talking about
trying the idea

00:52:01.630 --> 00:52:04.480 line:84%
for you guys of
creating art but not

00:52:04.480 --> 00:52:06.870 line:90%
ignoring where the world is.

00:52:06.870 --> 00:52:12.690 line:84%
And I guess, I really wonder
if this feels like it's--

00:52:12.690 --> 00:52:14.390 line:84%
there's actual
momentum behind these,

00:52:14.390 --> 00:52:16.250 line:84%
the changes that
we're seeing now,

00:52:16.250 --> 00:52:18.260 line:84%
because I thought about
this a couple weeks ago,

00:52:18.260 --> 00:52:20.390 line:84%
unfortunately, when
Chadwick Boseman passed,

00:52:20.390 --> 00:52:23.210 line:84%
that in the light of that
movie, where has there

00:52:23.210 --> 00:52:27.320 line:84%
been this search to say, well,
that worked less than something

00:52:27.320 --> 00:52:28.030 line:90%
else I've played.

00:52:28.030 --> 00:52:31.250 line:84%
It still feels to me like when
movies with casts of color

00:52:31.250 --> 00:52:36.560 line:84%
succeed, they feel anomalous,
rather than part of a wave.

00:52:36.560 --> 00:52:39.130 line:84%
And I just wonder how you
guys deal with that feeling

00:52:39.130 --> 00:52:41.030 line:90%
if [INAUDIBLE]?

00:52:41.030 --> 00:52:42.890 line:84%
Well, no matter how
much you might bring up

00:52:42.890 --> 00:52:47.870 line:84%
something that disproved
a notion, a film,

00:52:47.870 --> 00:52:51.920 line:84%
a particular film, or a
particular actor or whatever,

00:52:51.920 --> 00:52:55.265 line:84%
I don't know, it's like
people, they just, they forget.

00:52:58.100 --> 00:53:01.040 line:84%
You can quote situations where,
well, this movie did well

00:53:01.040 --> 00:53:03.740 line:84%
in Europe, even though it has
an all black cast, or whatever.

00:53:03.740 --> 00:53:07.190 line:84%
And it's like, [INAUDIBLE],, it's
not going to sell in Europe,

00:53:07.190 --> 00:53:08.270 line:90%
or whatever it is.

00:53:08.270 --> 00:53:09.830 line:84%
So I think there
are lessons learned

00:53:09.830 --> 00:53:11.772 line:84%
and then they're
quickly unlearned.

00:53:11.772 --> 00:53:13.730 line:84%
So I think it's kind of
a constant battle, kind

00:53:13.730 --> 00:53:16.368 line:90%
of going back--

00:53:16.368 --> 00:53:18.410 line:84%
you just need another
example and another example

00:53:18.410 --> 00:53:21.740 line:84%
and another example till
maybe something will stick.

00:53:21.740 --> 00:53:26.820 line:84%
But I don't think
that's always the case.

00:53:26.820 --> 00:53:28.150 line:90%
And it's an ongoing thing.

00:53:28.150 --> 00:53:30.530 line:84%
I mean, it's easy
right now to say

00:53:30.530 --> 00:53:35.750 line:84%
that what the Academy has
done is in a moment of,

00:53:35.750 --> 00:53:40.610 line:84%
just to put an example, it's in
a moment of Black Lives Matter.

00:53:40.610 --> 00:53:41.720 line:90%
But not really.

00:53:41.720 --> 00:53:43.560 line:84%
I mean, it's been, you
know, it's been five,

00:53:43.560 --> 00:53:47.510 line:84%
six, seven years since
the Academy has started

00:53:47.510 --> 00:53:49.820 line:84%
its inclusion with
membership and stuff,

00:53:49.820 --> 00:53:51.710 line:90%
because it is an ongoing thing.

00:53:51.710 --> 00:53:55.370 line:84%
I mean, the fact, the idea that
one thing is going to solve it,

00:53:55.370 --> 00:53:59.930 line:84%
you know, the Civil Rights
was 50 years ago plus.

00:53:59.930 --> 00:54:05.540 line:90%
So it's ongoing.

00:54:05.540 --> 00:54:10.670 line:84%
And pay attention,
because it's not

00:54:10.670 --> 00:54:14.570 line:84%
just what the Academy
is asking you to do.

00:54:14.570 --> 00:54:18.840 line:84%
But you know, don't be on
the wrong side of history.

00:54:18.840 --> 00:54:19.512 line:90%
Sure.

00:54:19.512 --> 00:54:21.470 line:84%
Julia, do you have any
thoughts about that too?

00:54:21.470 --> 00:54:24.360 line:84%
I just wondered what
it feels like to you?

00:54:24.360 --> 00:54:26.040 line:90%
Well, I don't know how--

00:54:26.040 --> 00:54:28.500 line:84%
I mean, the survey
was a large span.

00:54:28.500 --> 00:54:31.260 line:84%
I think it was like
from '07 to recently.

00:54:31.260 --> 00:54:35.480 line:84%
And I don't really
agree with that survey,

00:54:35.480 --> 00:54:40.160 line:84%
because I see the changes around
me, having just watched the US

00:54:40.160 --> 00:54:46.310 line:84%
Open and seeing Naomi Osaka win,
a biracial woman representing

00:54:46.310 --> 00:54:48.860 line:84%
Japan, and happens
to live in LA.

00:54:48.860 --> 00:54:50.690 line:84%
And of course, Serena
opened the doors,

00:54:50.690 --> 00:54:53.030 line:90%
and that was like 29 years ago.

00:54:53.030 --> 00:54:55.250 line:84%
It's a predominantly
white sport,

00:54:55.250 --> 00:54:58.370 line:90%
and now there's so much color.

00:54:58.370 --> 00:54:59.750 line:90%
I think that it's--

00:54:59.750 --> 00:55:03.830 line:84%
I see it happening, and I
pooh pooh the naysayers.

00:55:03.830 --> 00:55:07.640 line:84%
I think that I see
representation on screen.

00:55:07.640 --> 00:55:10.500 line:84%
And I do think there's
more work to be done.

00:55:10.500 --> 00:55:13.080 line:84%
But I think we're headed
in the right direction.

00:55:13.080 --> 00:55:16.910 line:84%
And I do think things like
the Academy's requirements

00:55:16.910 --> 00:55:20.750 line:84%
hold us accountable, keep
the conversation going,

00:55:20.750 --> 00:55:23.700 line:84%
and panels like this keep
the conversation going.

00:55:23.700 --> 00:55:25.823 line:90%
And that's all a good thing.

00:55:25.823 --> 00:55:27.740 line:84%
Well, that's probably a
great point to end on.

00:55:27.740 --> 00:55:29.040 line:84%
Let me thank you guys
so much for doing this.

00:55:29.040 --> 00:55:31.290 line:84%
I could really talk about
this for a whole lot longer,

00:55:31.290 --> 00:55:32.770 line:90%
but nobody [INAUDIBLE].

00:55:32.770 --> 00:55:33.560 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:33.560 --> 00:55:34.880 line:90%
Thank you guys so much.

00:55:34.880 --> 00:55:37.010 line:90%
Thanks, Elvis.

00:55:37.010 --> 00:55:39.260 line:90%
Hi, I'm Julia Kim.

00:55:39.260 --> 00:55:42.590 line:84%
Thank you for watching our
Academy Dialogues conversation.

00:55:42.590 --> 00:55:45.890 line:84%
Check out previous discussions,
and subscribe to the Oscars

00:55:45.890 --> 00:55:48.490 line:90%
channel for more videos.

